{"name":"Blooming Wisteria","hex":"#88777e","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blooming","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of bloom"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Opening in blossoms; flowering.","Thriving in health, beauty, and vigor, vigour; indicating the freshness and beauties of youth or health."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blooming"},{"term":"wisteria","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several woody climbing vines, of the genus Wisteria, native to the East Asian countries of China, Korea, and Japan and the eastern United States.","The lavenderish color of their flowers; specifically:"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wisteria"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wisteria","blocks":[{"text":"Wisteria is a genus of flowering plants in the legume family, Fabaceae (Leguminosae). The genus includes four species of woody twining vines that are native to China, Japan, Korea, Vietnam, southern Canada, the Eastern United States, and north of Iran. They were later introduced to England, France, Germany and various other countries in Europe. Some species are popular ornamental plants. The genus name is also used as the English name, and may then be spelt 'wistaria'."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Chinese_Wisteria_Bl%C3%BCtentrauben.JPG/330px-Chinese_Wisteria_Bl%C3%BCtentrauben.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wisteria","wikidata":"Q261903"},{"title":"Lavender (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Lavender is a light shade of purple or violet. It applies particularly to the color of the flower of the same name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Lavandula_fields.jpg/330px-Lavandula_fields.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lavender_(color)","wikidata":"Q2468392"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Wisteria_floribunda_MHNT.BOT.2008.1.38.jpg/500px-Wisteria_floribunda_MHNT.BOT.2008.1.38.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Wisteria_floribunda_MHNT.BOT.2008.1.38.jpg/1280px-Wisteria_floribunda_MHNT.BOT.2008.1.38.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Wisteria_floribunda_MHNT.BOT.2008.1.38.jpg/500px-Wisteria_floribunda_MHNT.BOT.2008.1.38.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Seeds and seedpods of Wisteria floribunda (Japanese wisteria). The seeds of all Wisteria species contain high levels of the wisterin toxin and are especially poisonous.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWisteria_floribunda_MHNT.BOT.2008.1.38.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Wisteria%E2%80%93IMG_5778~3.jpg/500px-Wisteria%E2%80%93IMG_5778~3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Wisteria%E2%80%93IMG_5778~3.jpg/1280px-Wisteria%E2%80%93IMG_5778~3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Wisteria%E2%80%93IMG_5778~3.jpg/500px-Wisteria%E2%80%93IMG_5778~3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wisteria, Turkey","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWisteria%E2%80%93IMG_5778~3.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/%E8%B6%B3%E5%88%A9%E3%81%AE%E8%97%A4_%28Wisteria_trellises_in_Ashikaga%29_29_Apr%2C_2009_-_panoramio.jpg/500px-%E8%B6%B3%E5%88%A9%E3%81%AE%E8%97%A4_%28Wisteria_trellises_in_Ashikaga%29_29_Apr%2C_2009_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=en